### 4. Behavioral Capital: A New Asset Class

Financial auditors evaluate financial capital. The AI Behavior Auditor evaluates **Behavioral Capital** — a quantitative measure of accumulated trustworthiness that functions as a genuine asset on the deploying organization's books.

Behavioral capital $\mathcal{B}_t$ evolves as a dynamic process over the deployment period:

$$\mathcal{B}_t = \mathcal{B}_{t-1} + \sum_{i} w_i \cdot \text{Credit}_i(t) - \sum_{j} w_j \cdot \text{Debit}_j(t)$$

Credit events increase behavioral capital:

$$\text{Credits:} \quad \{\text{audit passed}, \text{calibration stable}, \text{coherence maintained}, \text{no fairness violations}\}$$

Debit events decrease it:

$$\text{Debits:} \quad \{\text{incident occurred}, \text{fairness violation detected}, \text{hallucination rate increased}, \text{coherence failure observed}\}$$

The weights $w_i$ and $w_j$ are determined by the materiality class of each event. A Class IV critical incident produces a larger debit than a Class I trivial anomaly.

Behavioral capital serves three professional functions. First, it provides a continuous summary of the system's trustworthiness history, analogous to a credit history. Second, it feeds directly into the Behavioral Rating System described in the next section. Third, it creates a financially meaningful incentive for deploying organizations: a system with high behavioral capital is worth more, and its deployers have a quantifiable stake in maintaining it.

