### 8. Institutional Architecture of the Profession

Mathematics alone does not make a profession. Financial auditing became trustworthy because auditors are independent. Medicine became trustworthy because physicians are licensed. Law became trustworthy because lawyers are accountable to courts. The AI Behavior Auditor requires equivalent institutional infrastructure.

**The Independence Principle.** The auditor cannot certify a system they built, trained, or financially benefit from. Cannot be employed by the deploying organization during the audit period. Must disclose all conflicts of interest. Must maintain independence from audit outcomes — a finding of non-compliance must carry no financial penalty for the auditor beyond reputational consequences. Without independence, the profession produces certificates that institutions cannot trust and courts cannot rely on.

**Audit Liability.** Professional authority requires personal accountability. The dissertation develops the formal structure of auditor liability: negligence standards defining the duty of care owed by a credentialed auditor; certification standards defining what constitutes a competent audit; and legal responsibility boundaries defining when an auditor bears responsibility for harm caused by a system they certified. This is where the profession becomes legally real. An auditor who issues a compliant certificate for a system that subsequently causes Class IV material harm must face professional and potentially legal consequences — otherwise the certificate carries no weight.

**Auditability-by-Design.** The most powerful long-run contribution of the profession may not be auditing systems after they are built. It may be establishing standards that require systems to be buildable for auditing — the AI equivalent of building codes that require electrical systems to be inspectable. AI systems deployed in regulated domains should expose behavioral logs, uncertainty traces, decision provenance records, and audit hooks as standard engineering requirements. The dissertation develops the formal specification of **Auditable AI Architecture**: a set of design requirements that make continuous assurance technically feasible rather than heroically difficult.

