Appendix A: Continuous Assurance – E‑Processes and Sequential Validity

A.1 E‑Process Definition and Ville’s Inequality

An e‑process for a null hypothesis H_0 is a sequence of non‑negative random variables \{E_t\}_{t \ge 1} adapted to a filtration \{\mathcal{F}_t\} such that, under any distribution P \in H_0,

\mathbb{E}_P[E_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] \le E_{t-1}, \qquad E_0 = 1.

Thus \{E_t\} is a supermartingale with initial value 1.
Ville’s inequality (Ville, 1939) gives the anytime‑valid guarantee:

P\!\left(\exists t \ge 1 : E_t \ge \frac{1}{\alpha}\right) \le \alpha \quad \text{for all } \alpha \in (0,1).

This property makes e‑processes the natural building block for Continuous Behavioral Assurance – a certificate that remains valid at any stopping time, without pre‑specifying a sample size.

Example e‑process. For testing a simple null P_0 against alternative P_1 with densities p_0, p_1, the likelihood ratio process

E_t = \prod_{i=1}^t \frac{p_1(y_i)}{p_0(y_i)}

is a non‑negative martingale under P_0 (hence an e‑process). If the true data‑generating measure is P_0, Ville’s inequality controls the probability that E_t ever exceeds 1/\alpha.

A.2 The Continuous Assurance Certificate as a Stochastic Process

The certificate \mathcal{C}_t at time t is the tuple

\mathcal{C}_t = \bigl(\phi,\ \mathcal{R},\ \alpha_t,\ T,\ \mathcal{M}_t,\ \mathcal{B}_t,\ \text{Rating}_t\bigr)

where:

· \phi is the behavioral property under audit,
· \mathcal{R} is the region of the input‑output space under surveillance,
· \alpha_t is the current significance level (updated as evidence accumulates),
· T is the transformation class for coherence auditing,
· \mathcal{M}_t is the audit methodology state (e.g., chosen e‑process, thresholds),
· \mathcal{B}_t is the current behavioral capital,
· \text{Rating}_t is the current behavioral rating.

The certificate is living: its components are updated every time a new observation arrives, using the e‑process machinery.

A.3 CUSUM‑Based Behavioral Drift Detection

For LLM output monitoring we model the generation y_t as drawn from a categorical distribution. Let p_0 be the reference distribution under “normal” behavior and p_1 the post‑change distribution capturing a specific drift of interest (e.g., increased hallucination rate, fairness violation).
Define the log‑likelihood ratio increment

\ell_t = \log\frac{\hat{p}_1(y_t)}{\hat{p}_0(y_t)},

where \hat{p}_0,\hat{p}_1 are estimates based on reference data. The CUSUM statistic is

S_0 = 0,\qquad S_t = \bigl(S_{t-1} + \ell_t\bigr)^+,

and the drift alarm time is

\tau = \inf\{t \ge 1 : S_t \ge h\}.

Classical performance (known densities). Under P_0, the average run length satisfies

\mathbb{E}_\infty[\tau] \ge e^{h},

and Lorden’s detection delay under P_1 is bounded by

\mathbb{E}_1[\tau] \le \frac{h}{D_{\text{KL}}(p_1 \| p_0)} + O(1).

A.4 Theoretical Guarantees Under Estimated Densities

Theorem A.1 (Estimated CUSUM). Suppose \hat{p}_0,\hat{p}_1 are estimated from i.i.d. samples of size m and satisfy, with probability at least 1-\delta,

\|\hat{p}_0 - p_0\|_\infty \le \epsilon_m, \qquad \|\hat{p}_1 - p_1\|_\infty \le \epsilon_m,

with \epsilon_m = O(\sqrt{(\log m)/m}). Then there exists a threshold h(\gamma,m) such that

\mathbb{E}_\infty[\tau] \ge \gamma - C\sqrt{\frac{\log \gamma}{m}},

and the detection delay under p_1 increases by at most O(\sqrt{\log \gamma/m}) relative to the known‑density case.

Proof sketch. The e‑process formed by the estimated likelihood ratios deviates from the true martingale by an error term that is bounded in L^2 by O(1/m). Ville’s inequality applied to the perturbed process yields the adjusted ARL bound after optimizing the threshold h. Full details follow from standard change‑of‑measure arguments and concentration inequalities; see Appendix A.5 for the complete derivation.

