Appendix B: Dynamic Fairness Certification Under Covariate Shift

B.1 Problem Setting

Let A \in \{0,1\} be a protected attribute and Y = f(X) the model output. The fairness constraint (demographic parity) is

\bigl|\mathbb{P}(f(X)=1 \mid A=0) - \mathbb{P}(f(X)=1 \mid A=1)\bigr| \le \epsilon_{\text{fair}} .

We observe a stream of pairs (X_t, A_t, Y_t). The input distribution \mathcal{D}_t may shift over time (covariate shift). Our goal is an anytime‑valid test that detects violations of the fairness constraint without falsely alarming due to covariate shift alone.

B.2 Importance‑Weighted E‑Process

Let \mathcal{D}_0 be a reference distribution (e.g., the training distribution). Define the density ratio

w_t(x) = \frac{d\mathcal{D}_t}{d\mathcal{D}_0}(x),

assumed to exist and be bounded: w_t(x) \le W < \infty.
Under the null hypothesis H_0 of demographic parity, the importance‑weighted outcome probabilities are equal across groups. Construct the e‑process

E_t^w = \prod_{i=1}^t \frac{\hat{p}_1^w(Y_i \mid A_i)}{\hat{p}_0^w(Y_i \mid A_i)},

where for a given group a,

\hat{p}_a^w(Y \mid A=a) = \frac{\sum_{j: A_j=a} w_j(X_j) \mathbf{1}[Y_j=Y]}{\sum_{j: A_j=a} w_j(X_j)}.

The numerator uses a “post‑change” model where the conditional outcome distribution has a fairness violation of a specified form; the denominator represents the null model (equal outcome rates after reweighting). We set the reference distribution to the initial time window.

Theorem B.1 (Validity under covariate shift). Under H_0, if the density ratios are bounded and the conditional distribution of Y \mid A is invariant after reweighting, \{E_t^w\} is a supermartingale, hence

P_{H_0}\!\left(\exists t \ge 1 : E_t^w \ge 1/\alpha\right) \le \alpha.

Proof. Under H_0, \mathbb{E}_{P_0}[ (p_1^w/p_0^w) \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}, A_t ] = 1 because the weighted distribution of Y given A matches the null model. The boundedness of w_t guarantees the likelihood ratio is integrable. The product form then yields a martingale. Ville’s inequality completes the proof.

B.3 Estimation of Density Ratios

In practice w_t is estimated using probabilistic classifiers trained on windows of data. The bound W is replaced by an empirical maximum, and the e‑process can be adjusted by a “shrinkage” factor to maintain conservative validity. The finite‑sample effect is studied in Section B.4.

