Appendix C: Conformal Hallucination Auditing

C.1 Coverage‑Based Definition

Given a nonconformity score s(x,y) (e.g., 1 - \text{softmax probability of the true output}), a conformal predictor constructs a prediction set

C_\alpha(x) = \{ y : s(x,y) \le \hat{q}_{1-\alpha} \},

where \hat{q}_{1-\alpha} is the (1-\alpha)-quantile of scores on a calibration set. Under exchangeability, \mathbb{P}(Y_{\text{new}} \in C_\alpha(X_{\text{new}})) \ge 1-\alpha (Vovk et al., 2005).

C.2 Sequential Coverage Monitoring

Define the cumulative deviation process

M_0 = 0,\qquad M_t = M_{t-1} + \bigl(\mathbf{1}[Y_t \in C_\alpha(X_t)] - (1-\alpha)\bigr).

Under correct calibration, the increments are i.i.d. with mean zero and bounded support [- (1-\alpha), \alpha]. A test martingale can be formed via Hoeffding’s inequality:

E_t = \exp\!\Bigl(\lambda M_t - \frac{\lambda^2}{8} t\Bigr), \qquad \lambda > 0,

which is a supermartingale. An alarm is raised if E_t \ge 1/\tilde{\alpha}. The detection speed is governed by the Kullback–Leibler divergence between the actual coverage rate and the nominal rate.

Theorem C.1 (Coverage drift detection). If the true coverage drops to 1-\alpha - \delta, the expected detection delay is

\mathbb{E}[\tau] \le \frac{-\log \tilde{\alpha}}{2\delta^2} + O(1).

Proof. Standard from properties of the CUSUM/log‑SPRT for Bernoulli observations.

C.3 Hallucination Definition for Generative Models

For LLM auditing, we define hallucination as the event that the model output Y is factually inconsistent with a ground‑truth knowledge base \mathcal{K}. The set C_\alpha(x) is taken as the set of responses y that are consistent with \mathcal{K} according to a verification function. The conformal score s(x,y) measures the model’s internal uncertainty and is calibrated to achieve marginal coverage of the factual correctness event. This extension is detailed in Appendix C.4.

