Appendix E: Multi‑Agent Auditing

E.1 System Model

A multi‑agent system \mathcal{M} = (N, E) consists of agents i \in N with policies f_i, connected by communication edges E. The global behavior emerges from sequential interactions.

E.2 Collusive Failure

Definition E.1 (Collusive failure). A subset S \subseteq N exhibits collusive failure with respect to property \phi if

\prod_{i \in S} f_i \not\models \phi \quad \text{but} \quad \forall i \in S,\ f_i \models \phi.

The failure arises from the interaction structure, not from any single agent’s violation.

Audit approach. The auditor simulates the multi‑agent rollout on a representative set of scenarios and checks the global property \phi. If a violation is found, blame attribution is performed using Shapley‑value analysis on the joint policy.

E.3 Hallucination Amplification

In a chain of agents, errors can compound. If each agent introduces an independent hallucination probability p, the overall error rate after k steps grows as 1-(1-p)^k. The auditor certifies the effective hallucination rate of the ensemble, accounting for possible error propagation topologies.

E.4 Emergent Safety Properties

Formal verification of emergent behavior: the auditor attempts to verify that the composition of individually verified agents preserves or violates a global safety invariant. We provide a compositional proof rule in Appendix E.5, using assume‑guarantee contracts.

