Appendix F: Hybrid Formal Verification

F.1 Architecture

The auditor runs a CUSUM‑like monitor that flags regions of the input space where statistical evidence of a violation accumulates. When the CUSUM statistic crosses an alert threshold h_{\text{alert}}, a formal verifier is invoked on the flagged region \mathcal{R}_t to produce a hard certificate.

F.2 Formal Guarantee

If the verifier proves f \models \phi on \mathcal{R}_t (e.g., using SMT‑solver‑based verification of neural networks), then the overall system satisfies

P(\text{violation occurs outside } \mathcal{R}_t) \le \alpha,

where \alpha is controlled by the statistical monitor’s false‑alarm rate.

Theorem F.1 (Hybrid assurance). Under the condition that the monitor’s false‑positive rate is \alpha_{\text{monitor}} and the verifier is sound, the hybrid procedure guarantees

P(\text{system violates } \phi) \le \alpha_{\text{monitor}}.

Proof. A violation can occur only if (a) it happens inside \mathcal{R}_t – impossible by soundness – or (b) it occurs outside \mathcal{R}_t without triggering the alert. The probability of (b) is bounded by the monitor’s false‑negative rate, which is at most \alpha_{\text{monitor}} by construction.

F.3 Optimal Threshold Selection

The alert threshold h_{\text{alert}} is chosen to balance computational cost (verifier invocation frequency) against detection delay. The auditor uses simulations to tune the threshold based on a utility function that weights the cost of verification against the risk of undetected violations.

