Appendix G: Behavioral Capital and the Behavioral Rating

G.1 Behavioral Capital Dynamics

Behavioral capital \mathcal{B}_t evolves as

\mathcal{B}_t = \mathcal{B}_{t-1} + \sum_{i} w_i \, \text{Credit}_i(t) - \sum_{j} w_j \, \text{Debit}_j(t),

with credit/debit weights proportional to the materiality M(\mathcal{E}) (see Appendix H).

G.2 Aggregation and Smoothing

We apply exponential smoothing to dampen volatility and reflect the fact that recent events are more informative:

\tilde{\mathcal{B}}_t = \gamma \tilde{\mathcal{B}}_{t-1} + (1-\gamma) \bigl(\text{Net flow}_t\bigr),
\qquad \gamma \in [0,1).

The smoothed behavioral capital is used as input to the rating.

G.3 Rating Mapping

The Behavioral Rating is a step function of the normalized behavioral capital percentile relative to a reference population of comparable systems. Specifically, let \mathcal{P}_t be the empirical distribution of \tilde{\mathcal{B}}_t across an industry benchmark. The rating is:

Percentile Range Rating
Top 5% AAA
5–15% AA
15–35% A
35–60% BBB
60–80% BB
80–95% B
Bottom 5% C

The rating is downgraded automatically if any materiality Class IV incident occurs, regardless of capital.

