Appendix H: AI Materiality Theory – Formalization

H.1 Materiality Measure

An error event \mathcal{E} is a tuple (harm occurrence, severity, scale). We model the expected harm as a random variable H(\mathcal{E}) with distribution estimated from historical data and expert elicitation. The materiality is

M(\mathcal{E}) = \mathbb{E}[H(\mathcal{E})] = \mathbb{P}(\text{harm} \mid \mathcal{E}) \times \mathbb{E}[\text{severity} \mid \mathcal{E}] \times \mathbb{E}[\text{scale} \mid \mathcal{E}],

where severity is measured on a normalized 0–1 scale (0 = no harm, 1 = maximum foreseeable harm), and scale is the number of affected individuals divided by a reference population.

H.2 Threshold Calibration

Regulatory bodies prescribe a materiality function g(M) that maps materiality to required audit action. The thresholds \epsilon_1, \epsilon_2, \epsilon_3 are solutions to g(M) = \text{cost of action}. For example, in the healthcare domain, a Class IV critical threshold might be \epsilon_3 = 10^{-6} expected QALY loss per patient‑year.

H.3 Materiality‑Weighted Audit Sampling

The auditor allocates test effort proportionally to materiality. Given a total test budget N, samples are drawn from regions of the input space with higher expected materiality, using importance sampling with density proportional to M(\mathcal{E}(x)). The auditor’s opinion is then based on a materiality‑weighted error rate.

