Appendix I: The Behavioral Balance Sheet – Construction and Weights

I.1 Asset and Liability Definitions

Assets (higher is better):

· Calibration quality \mathcal{K}_t: 1 – expected calibration error (ECE).
· Coherence index \mathcal{C}(f, \mathcal{T}).
· Fairness compliance 1 - \max_{\text{groups}} |\text{DP}|.
· Adversarial robustness \mathcal{C}_{\text{adv}}(f,\epsilon).
· Formal compliance: 1 if verified, else 0 (or fraction of verified safety properties).
· Behavioral capital \tilde{\mathcal{B}}_t normalized to [0,1].

Liabilities (lower is better):

· Drift risk \mathbb{P}(\tau < T_{\text{audit}}) estimated from CUSUM performance.
· Bias exposure \max_{\text{groups}} |\text{DP}|.
· Hallucination rate 1 - \text{empirical coverage}.
· Adversarial vulnerability 1 - \mathcal{C}_{\text{adv}}(f,\epsilon).
· Materiality‑weighted incident count \sum M(\mathcal{E}_j).

I.2 Net Behavioral Integrity

\text{NBI}_t = \sum_{i} v_i \cdot \text{Asset}_i - \sum_{j} w_j \cdot \text{Liability}_j.

The weights v_i, w_j are set by the auditor’s professional judgment and may be domain‑specific. A default set recommended by the profession’s standards body is derived from a principal component analysis of historical audit outcomes against realized system performance. The NBI is then mapped to the rating via a monotonic transformation calibrated to the population of rated systems.

